// Skill landing page — becomes SKILL.md in the `wcl_skill` (`:ai_skill`)
// build. It belongs only to the skill site; the reference pages it links to
// are shared from the `reference` book, so each link resolves to
// `references/<page>.md`. Authored agent-facing (what WCL is, when to reach
// for this skill, where each topic lives, and how to validate the result).
page wcl_skill_home { sites = [:wcl_skill]  start = true  title = "WCL"
  h1 "Authoring WCL"

  p "WCL is a typed configuration language built around **fields** (`name = value`) and **blocks** (`kind \"label\" { … }`), with a real type system, first-class functions, pattern matching, and a schema layer that validates a document's structure. `wdoc` — the static-site / document generator these references are written in — is one WCL application built on that base."

  callout "When to use this skill" {
    class = ["info"]
    body  = "Reach for it whenever you write or edit `.wcl` files: authoring or reading WCL configuration, defining `type` / `interface` / `union` schemas and `@decorator`s, calling builtins, or building wdoc documentation. The `references/` folder is the full language, builtin, CLI and wdoc reference — consult the page for the feature you're touching before guessing syntax."
  }

  h2 "Validate your work"

  p "WCL has a CLI. After writing or editing a document, check it and format it:"

  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl check path/to/file.wcl      # parse + schema-validate; reports diagnostics
wcl fmt path/to/file.wcl        # canonical formatting (use --check in CI)
wcl get path/to/file.wcl field  # evaluate and print one field's value
wcl eval path/to/file.wcl       # evaluate the whole document to JSON
BASH
  }

  p "The full command set — `parse`, `check`, `eval` / `get`, `set`, `fmt`, `repl`, `lsp`, and the `wdoc` subcommands — is in the [CLI reference](cli)."

  h2 "Language reference"

  p "One page per feature. Start with [fields & blocks](fields_blocks) (the core shape) and [schema & decorators](schema) (how documents are typed and validated)."

  list {
    li "[Fields & blocks](fields_blocks) — the `name = value` / `kind \"label\" { … }` foundation"
    li "[Tables](tables) — the `@table` row shorthand for repeated blocks"
    li "[Identifiers](identifiers) — what may be a name"
    li "Types: [numbers](numbers), [strings](strings), [booleans](booleans), [symbols](symbols), [optionals](optionals), [lists](lists), [tensors](tensors), [records](records)"
    li "[Interfaces](interfaces), [references](references) (`&T`) and [unions](unions) — structural typing and variants"
    li "[Connections](connections) — typed relationships between blocks"
    li "[Expressions](expressions), [functions](functions) and [control flow](control_flow) — computation, `fn` literals, pattern matching"
    li "[Schema & decorators](schema) — `@document` / `@block` / `@child(ren)` / `@inline` / `@default` / `@table` and the type system"
    li "[Imports & modules](imports) — quoted disk imports and `<angle-bracket>` system imports"
  }

  h2 "Builtins & CLI"

  list {
    li "[Builtin functions](builtins) — collections, strings, lists, math, tensors and control-flow helpers"
    li "[CLI reference](cli) — every `wcl` subcommand and its flags"
  }

  h2 "wdoc (documentation generator)"

  p "If the task involves building docs, sites, PDFs or skills with WCL, start at the [wdoc overview](wdoc_overview). Key pages: [sites & templates](wdoc_sites), [pages](wdoc_pages), [formatting](wdoc_formatting), [diagrams](wdoc_diagrams), [Markdown output](wdoc_markdown) and [skill folders](wdoc_skills). The rest of the `references/` folder covers charts, timelines, terminals, maps, icons, images, math and styling."

  callout "Authoring tips" {
    class = ["tip"]
    body  = "Prefer the declared schema: bare record literals coerce to the matching union variant by shape. `let name = expr` items are composition helpers — resolvable by name but invisible to the document's fields / JSON / schema. When unsure of a builtin's signature or a block's fields, open its reference page rather than inferring."
  }
}
